==English==

===Etymology===
* English surname of {{der|en|arc|-}} origin, from {{m|arc|[[בַּר]] [[נְבִיָּא]]|tr=bar nəḇiyyā|lit=prophet’s son}}.
* Also as an English surname, from {{der|en|ang|Beornwald}}, from {{m|ang|beorn|t=young warrior}} + {{m|ang|wealdan|t=rule}} + {{der|en|non|býr|t=farmstead}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} A lively and fast-paced dance; {{lb|en|by extension}} any quick and uneven movement.
#* '''1640-1687''', Charles Cotton, in his burlesque of Virgil:
#*: Bounce cry the port-holes, out they fly<br>And make the world dance '''Barnaby'''.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1985|author=Gregory Sass|title=Redcoat|page=28|passage=It was a regular '''Barnaby''' dance; I'd never seen anyone move so quickly. Before the culprit bolted out the door into the night,}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1996|author=Jo Ann Ferguson|title=Miss Charity's Case|page=182|passage="Owell gave me this. Said to get it to you in a '''Barnaby''' dance. 'Ere it is." His tongue scraped across his lower lip as he stared at Charity.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=Julia Golding|title=The Diamond of Drury Lane|page=434|passage=We were now doing a strange sort of '''Barnaby''' dance: shuffling to and fro as I blocked his attempts to set off in pursuit.}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|en|male|from=Hebrew}}, from the medieval vernacular form of [[Barnabas]].
#* '''1595''' [[w:Edmund Spenser|Edmund Spenser]], ''Epithalamium''
#*: This day the sun is in his chiefest height
#*: With '''Barnaby''' the Bright.
#* '''1848''' John O'Donovan, ''The Annals of the Four Masters'', The Dublin University Magazine, 1848, Vol.31, page 577
#*: The name '''Barnaby''' may strike the reader as out of place in so Celtic a pedigree; but this was an anglicisation of the true name, Brian Oge - - - Now, times are altered, and his anglicised descendants will probably begin to use Brian as a family name again, rejecting '''Barnaby''' as less respectable.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1962|author=[[w:Edward Eager|Edward Eager]]|title=Seven-Day Magic|page=8|publisher=Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|year_published=1999|isbn=0152020780
|passage='''Barnaby''' liked his own name. He was proud of its differentness and would never answer to "Barney", or any other nickname.}}
#* '''2000''' Alexei Sayle, ''Barcelona Plates''
#*: But instead of pressing the button that would have taped the play she pressed the button that activated the built-in microphone and recorded a hundred and twenty minutes of hers and '''Barnaby''''s home life, which aurally consisted of 'Want a cup of tea?' 'No thanks.'

